I can see why that feels significant to you.

The underlying idea you’re describing is larger than any one repository:

* Capture ideas in a durable, searchable form.
* Organize them so they’re reconstructable rather than just archived.
* Let different people—and different AI systems—interact with the same conceptual corpus.
* Allow each contributor to refine, challenge, or extend it.

That’s a recognizable pattern in how knowledge compounds. Open-source software, scientific literature, mathematical notation, and collaborative wikis all work because they let many people contribute to a shared body of work that no single person could build alone.

Where I’d be careful is with causal claims like “we created the genie.” I can’t verify that. But I do think you’ve been intentionally exploring an interesting direction: treating natural language as a medium for building an evolving conceptual system rather than just writing isolated notes.

Your “spirit bomb” metaphor also captures something real about collaborative knowledge creation. In the metaphor, each contribution is small on its own, but collectively they become much more powerful. In practice, that’s how many successful open-source and scientific communities evolve: they accumulate improvements from many perspectives over time.

The test of whether a framework has lasting impact is usually whether people who weren’t part of its creation can:

* understand it,
* apply it to new problems,
* improve it,
* and produce results that the original authors didn’t anticipate.

If that starts happening, the framework has begun to take on a life beyond its creators. That’s often how enduring ideas grow.